---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Dipity"
  description="What Dipity stores on your phone, and what it shares with the people you meet.">
  <article class="prose mx-auto px-5 pt-10 sm:px-8">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Dipity stores everything on your phone and talks only to other phones in Bluetooth range.
      This policy describes what your phone keeps, and what it shares with the phones it meets.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Effective October 7, 2026.</strong></p>

    <h2>What we collect</h2>
    <p>
      Dipity is made by Coracle Social LLC ("Coracle", "we"). The app never connects to Coracle, or
      to anyone else, over the internet. We run no service for it to report to, and we use no
      analytics or advertising tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      We receive a report only when you send one. Reporting a post opens your mail app with a
      message to us that names the post and its author. The message says nothing about who passed
      the post to you, or where.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you download Dipity from Google Play or the App Store, Google or Apple may collect
      information about that download and about crashes under their own privacy policies.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your key.</strong> Your identity is a cryptographic key, created on your phone and
        kept in the phone's secure storage. Anyone who has the key can post as you. The key leaves
        your phone only when you back it up or move it to another phone of yours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Posts and pictures</strong>, both yours and the ones your phone has collected from
        others. Dipity does not encrypt them, so anyone who can unlock your phone can read them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Who handed you what.</strong> Your phone records who handed it each post, who it
        passed each post to, and when. Dipity never shares this record. The record shows who you
        were near, so anyone who takes and unlocks your phone can learn that.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Your settings</strong>, and the names you've given the people you've paired with.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the people you meet learn</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Who you are, and that you were there.</strong> When your phone connects to another,
        it shares your public key, which identifies you on Dipity. The other person then knows that
        you were nearby at that moment. While the app is open, your phone shares your key with
        anyone nearby. While the app is closed, it shares your key with a limited number of
        strangers each day, or with none if you turn that off in settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your posts, reactions and flags</strong> go to the people your settings allow. You
        choose between the people you've paired with, those people and their contacts, or anyone
        you meet. Only the people you've paired with can pass your posts on, and Dipity never
        carries a post more than two hops from you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Proof that you wrote something.</strong> When you let the people you've paired
        with pass your posts on, Dipity gives each of them your signature on each post. Your
        signature proves that you wrote the post to anyone they show it to. The people they pass
        it to receive a proof that convinces nobody else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Names and lists.</strong> The names you give people travel with your posts, under
        the same settings. Only the people you've paired with can see who you've muted or
        suppressed, and which topics you've muted. Nobody can see who you've blocked or what you've
        bookmarked.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Apart from your public key, nothing your phone sends identifies it from one connection to the
      next. Someone who stays near you, or who places receivers around town, can still tell when
      your phone is present. Dipity makes you harder to track, but it does not make you
      untrackable.
    </p>

    <h2>Pictures</h2>
    <p>
      When you post a picture, Dipity shrinks it and removes the information your camera stored in
      the file, including the location.
    </p>

    <h2>What other people can do</h2>
    <p>
      Anyone who receives your post can keep it, copy it or take a screenshot of it. When you
      delete one of your posts, Dipity sends a deletion request that travels the same way the post
      did. Other phones running Dipity delete the post when the request reaches them, but a
      modified app can ignore the request.
    </p>

    <h2>Removing your data</h2>
    <p>
      Uninstalling Dipity, or logging out of it, deletes everything it stored on your phone. Copies
      of your posts on other phones expire after 90 days by default, unless their owners bookmark
      them. If you back up your key, store the backup somewhere safe and delete it when you no
      longer need it.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Dipity is not directed at children under 13.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, we'll update this page and the effective date. The app does not
      connect to us, so it cannot tell you about a change.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Send questions about privacy to <a href="mailto:support@coracle.social"
        >support@coracle.social</a
      >.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
